{"version":"2026.10.11-r25","id":"Q2823290","formula":"CH₄O₄S","hill":"CH4O4S","elements":["H","C","O","S"],"composition":{"C":1.0,"H":4.0,"O":4.0,"S":1.0},"names":{"en":"Methyl bisulfate"},"descriptions":{"en":"Methyl bisulfate is a chemical compound with the molecular formula (CH3)HSO4. This compound is the mono-methyl ester of sulfuric acid. Its structure is CH3−O−S(=O)2−OH. The significance of methyl bisulfate is that it is an intermediate in the hydrolysis of the important reagent dimethyl sulfate, (CH3)2SO4: (CH3)2SO4 + H2O → (CH3)HSO4 + CH3OH Methyl bisulfate is a strong acid: (CH3)HSO4 → (CH3)SO−4 + H+ Methyl bisulfate came into the public view in 1993 with the discovery that certain mercury compounds catalyze the conversion of methane to methylbisulfate in good yields with excellent selectivity in concentrated sulfuric acid."},"fame":11,"pubchemCid":6412,"mass":112.11,"charge":0,"smiles":"COS(=O)(=O)O","inchikey":"JZMJDSHXVKJFKW-UHFFFAOYSA-N","iupac":"methyl hydrogen sulfate","classes":[{"qid":"Q113145171","names":{"en":"type of chemical entity","ru":"тип химического объекта","uk":"тип хімічної сутності"}}],"structureInfo":{"kind":"molecule","source":"pubchem","ref":"6412","atoms":10,"license":"PubChem (NIH), public data"},"structure":{"kind":"molecule","atoms":[{"x":0.452,"y":0.021,"z":-0.113,"el":"S"},{"x":-0.959,"y":-0.042,"z":0.711,"el":"O"},{"x":1.579,"y":-0.232,"z":1.038,"el":"O"},{"x":0.484,"y":-1.116,"z":-1.014,"el":"O"},{"x":0.635,"y":1.365,"z":-0.629,"el":"O"},{"x":-2.191,"y":0.004,"z":0.007,"el":"C"},{"x":-2.253,"y":0.921,"z":-0.586,"el":"H"},{"x":-3.008,"y":-0.005,"z":0.733,"el":"H"},{"x":-2.285,"y":-0.869,"z":-0.644,"el":"H"},{"x":1.472,"y":0.305,"z":1.858,"el":"H"}],"bonds":[[0,1,1],[0,2,1],[0,3,2],[0,4,2],[1,5,1],[2,9,1],[5,6,1],[5,7,1],[5,8,1]]},"sources":[{"role":"identity","name":"Wikidata","url":"https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q2823290","license":"CC0 1.0"},{"role":"description","name":"Wikipedia (en)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/